---
/**
 * M6 — Designing an AI-aware task. A different template shape: the artefact is a
 * task redesign (segmented before/after) plus a six-question checklist.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ModuleHeader from '../../components/ModuleHeader.astro';
import ModuleWorkbench from '../../components/ModuleWorkbench.tsx';
import { MODULES } from '../../lib/modules';
import { buildWorkbenchConfig } from '../../lib/module-content';
import '../../styles/workspace.css';

const m = MODULES.find((x) => x.slug === 'designing-ai-aware-tasks')!;
const config = buildWorkbenchConfig(m.num);
---

<Base
  title={m.title}
  description="Redesign a teaching task so the thinking stays the student’s — without banning AI."
  maxWidth="none"
>
  <div class="ws-main">
    <ModuleHeader label="Module 6" duration={m.duration!} title="Designing an AI-aware task" />

    <section class="ws-body">
      <div class="ws-cols">
        <!-- reading column (static) -->
        <div class="ws-reading">
          <p class="standfirst">
            You don’t need to ban it. You need to design so the thinking is still
            theirs.
          </p>
          <div class="ws-prose">
            <p>
              The panic move is to forbid AI and police the result. It rarely
              works, it corrodes trust, and it puts you in an arms race you can’t
              win. The calmer move is older than AI: design the task so that doing
              the thinking is the only way through it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Take a task you already set. Find where a model walks straight
              through it — then redesign so an AI-only answer is visibly thin, and
              a student’s own reading becomes the shortcut rather than the
              obstacle.
            </p>
            <p>
              Work on the right: flip the task between how it stands and a
              redesign, then hold your new version against the six checklist
              questions.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pull-quote">
            <p>
              “The trick wasn’t catching AI. It was setting work where a shortcut
              left nothing worth handing in.”
            </p>
            <p>— a foundation-year tutor, after one redesign</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- workbench rail (island) -->
        <ModuleWorkbench client:load config={config} />
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
